            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Karla Cooks"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I like to fry an egg in the bacon grease and add it to the \"classic.\" I call it a BLTE.",
            "datePublished": "2012-08-02T08:42:22.483Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "DeeLee"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Thia is the way I have alwayus made my BLT' s onlyn varyingn the kind of bread sometimes. So good. Lately to make it healthier I havesubstituted baby Spinch leaves for lettuce and also sometimes substitute garlic hummus for mayo All are good. BLT's were my craving all through my first pregnancy and I would eat 2 or 3 at a time. I was always hungry.",
            "datePublished": "2026-02-10T22:43:30.170Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "candysmom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Your basic, straight-forward BLT recipe.\nThe one thing I will add, and this is from being a long-time cook, is when you assemble the sandwich, after spreading the mayo on your toast clear to the edge, place the lettuce down on your toast, then the tomato in the MIDDLE of your sandwich. This helps keep your sandwich from becoming soggy. Our all-time favorite summer sandwich, since the early 1950's.",
            "datePublished": "2021-08-10T03:59:10.723Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "PBFITZ"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a great standard recipe. The key for this sandwich is the quality of the ingredients. (Home Grown Tomatoes). My favorite BLT sandwich came from a restaurant up in Carmel, ca. I found out that they notched it up with a very thin layer of peanut butter, on one slice of the bread, and between the lettuce and tomatoes, they added thinly sliced Maui onion. Since I am not a peanut butter fan, I can't believe how much I love this sandwich. But I do.",
            "datePublished": "2010-08-20T13:56:30.600Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "France C"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Agree, it's nothing fancy, but sometimes those are the best recipes! I usually use more than 2 slices of tomato for my tastes, but adjust to how you like it! A sprinkle of salt and pepper on the tomato and you're good to go!",
            "datePublished": "2023-11-27T02:54:59.144Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "bellepepper"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This truly is a classic! But it’s at its best when homegrown tomatoes are available. Well, it’s that time of year in Ohio right now, so I made this using a beautiful Ohio River Valley tomato, bright green baby lettuce from Hubs’ garden and some high quality bacon cooked to perfection. Instead of the usual bread, this time I made it on a toasted “everything” bagel. And, don't forget the mayo! Just delicious!!",
            "datePublished": "2011-08-24T19:29:49.270Z"
          },
